title: Overview
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

Use these recommendations to agree on a team workflow, record interpretable observations, and check that transfers and backups are usable. Apply your institution's collection and data policies alongside them.

Each page gives a worked example and names the evidence behind it, so you can judge whether a recommendation applies to your project rather than taking it on trust. Where a statement simply describes how NAHPU behaves, it is presented as software behavior and carries no citation.

{%aside type="caution" title="Important Notice"%}
The pages in this section are still work in progress. They are not yet complete, and may contain errors or omissions. Please check back later for updates.
{% /aside %}

New to NAHPU? Start with [Day One](/en/day-one/) to learn the concepts in a practice project. The [Usage guides](/en/usages/) explain individual fields and controls; the [Cookbook](/en/how-to-recipes/) provides task instructions.

- [App Setup](/en/best-practices/app-setup/): configure and rehearse on every device.
- [Project Management](/en/best-practices/project-management/): assign responsibilities, coordinate identifiers, and manage transfers.
- [Data Collection](/en/best-practices/data-collection/): name sites and coordinates, georeference specimens, and document observations.
- [Data Management and Specimen Curation](/en/best-practices/data-management/): review records, protect files, and prepare exports and labels.
- [Device Choice and Care](/en/best-practices/device-care/): choose field-tested equipment, and plan power, storage, cleaning, and offline work.

Review these arrangements when people, devices, methods, or project requirements change. Keep the agreed protocol and unresolved issues with the project documentation.
